<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Grave Circle A</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1600–1500 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century-bce">17th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Grave Circle A is a royal cemetery just south of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lion-gate">Lion Gate</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mycenae">Mycenae</a> in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>, a circle 27.5 metres across with six shaft graves used from about 1600 to 1500 BCE. Its nineteen burials of men, women and children held weapons, jewellery, gold and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> cups and gold death masks, among them the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mask-of-agamemnon">Mask of Agamemnon</a>, with an estimated 15 kilograms of gold in all. First outside the walls, it was enclosed in the citadel when the fortifications were extended about 1250 BCE. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heinrich-schliemann">Heinrich Schliemann</a> and Panagiotis Stamatakis excavated it in 1876–1877.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/grave-circle-a.jpg" alt="Grave Circle A" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Dennis G. Jarvis · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Greece-0388_(2215117229).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Mycenaean shaft graves were an Argive form of the Middle Helladic burial tradition. Grave Circle A appears to continue the older Grave Circle B and reflects a wider trend in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> towards greater spending on burials; it may at first have been reserved for men. The shaft graves mark the rise of a local Greek-speaking royal dynasty whose wealth rested on long-distance sea trade. Mounds were raised over the graves and stelae set up on them, three of which show <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chariot">chariot</a> scenes. After the last burial the rulers turned to a new and grander type of tomb, the tholos, already developing in Messenia.</p><p>When the circle was taken inside the walls, a double ring wall was built around it and the ground levelled into a precinct for ceremonies, with the stelae set up again and possibly an altar over one grave. Archaeologists read this as an attempt by the 13th-century BCE rulers to claim the heroic past of an older dynasty.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The nineteen dead were eight men, nine women and two children. Each shaft held two to five bodies except Grave II, a single burial, and the graves ranged from 1 to 4 metres deep. Men were buried with weapons, above all swords, and women with jewellery. Five gold masks came from Graves IV and V, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mask-of-agamemnon">Mask of Agamemnon</a> from Grave V, and the finds include the Silver Siege Rhyton, boars' tusks, rings, buttons and bracelets.</p><p>Decorated daggers that were works of art rather than real weapons, ornate staffs and a sceptre from Grave IV marked high rank. Most objects follow the Minoan style, such as bulls' heads with a double axe, though the fighting and hunting scenes are Mycenaean, an example of the so-called international style.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Discovery and study</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mycenae">Mycenae</a> was the first site in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> to be excavated by modern methods. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heinrich-schliemann">Heinrich Schliemann</a> was led there by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iliad">Iliad</a>, which calls Mycenae abounding in gold, and by Pausanias, who reported a local tradition that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agamemnon">Agamemnon</a> lay within the citadel with his charioteer Eurymedon and the two children of Cassandra. Schliemann cleared five shafts, took them for the graves Pausanias described and stopped; a year later Stamatakis found the sixth.</p><p>The burials are now known to be some three centuries older than the traditional date of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trojan-war">Trojan War</a>, so they cannot be <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agamemnon">Agamemnon</a>'s. Some have suggested they belonged to an earlier ruling house, the Perseids of Greek myth.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Grave Circle A, Mycenae” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>At Mycenae two circles of deep shaft graves, Grave Circle B, begun before 1600 BCE, and <b>Grave Circle A</b>, of about 1600–1500 BCE, held men, women and children buried with gold masks, diadems and cups, bronze swords, daggers inlaid with hunting scenes in gold and silver, Baltic amber…</blockquote><a href="/culture/mycenaean-civilisation">Mycenaean civilisation · Origins</a></li></ul></section>
